1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:02,086
These are muscle cars.

2
00:00:02,087 --> 00:00:11,000
400 cubic inches, horsepower to match, and enough torque to leave the competition in the dust.

3
00:00:11,001 --> 00:00:20,000
Welcome to the world of power and performance, not made for professional races, but for average drivers, young and old, whose racetracks are the streets.

4
00:00:20,001 --> 00:00:23,000
Now, muscle cars on modern markets.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:44,000
To a muscle car owner, this is what it's all about.

6
00:00:45,320 --> 00:00:48,880
Putting together the magic combination of a powerful engine,

7
00:00:49,480 --> 00:00:52,600
carburetors in two, four, or even six packs,

8
00:00:53,280 --> 00:00:56,360
and just enough secret sauce in the head design

9
00:00:56,361 --> 00:01:01,480
to blow away any car that dared revit's engine at a stoplight.

10
00:01:03,940 --> 00:01:07,200
It's hard to believe, but in the early 60s,

11
00:01:07,540 --> 00:01:11,620
most of that muscle car magic could be bought right off the showroom floor.

12
00:01:12,340 --> 00:01:17,000
All of the big three, General Motors, Chrysler, and Ford,

13
00:01:17,120 --> 00:01:22,300
sold high-performance cars that ran like race cars and looked even better.

14
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:24,840
There's no other car quite like it.

15
00:01:24,841 --> 00:01:27,020
Ford barreling for 63.

16
00:01:29,240 --> 00:01:32,500
A muscle car was greater than the sum of its parts.

17
00:01:33,300 --> 00:01:35,780
Sure, it had to have a very powerful V8 engine.

18
00:01:36,240 --> 00:01:38,500
It had to have an aggressive exterior.

19
00:01:39,180 --> 00:01:42,680
It had to have a wonderful, menacing exhaust tone.

20
00:01:44,920 --> 00:01:46,980
It just had to be in your face.

21
00:01:46,981 --> 00:01:54,980
If somebody questioned you, often times it was the modern equivalent of the Old West

22
00:01:54,981 --> 00:01:56,980
shootout on Main Street at noon.

23
00:01:56,981 --> 00:02:01,980
You went out, you saw what your car did compared to somebody else's.

24
00:02:01,981 --> 00:02:03,980
That could settle a lot of arguments.

25
00:02:07,980 --> 00:02:13,980
Ask 10 Autobuffs what they consider the first muscle car, and you're likely to get 10 different answers.

26
00:02:13,981 --> 00:02:20,280
Ask what's the best muscle car and you're likely to end up in the middle of a street brawl.

27
00:02:21,280 --> 00:02:25,320
Whatever the favorite, there's probably an example enjoying retirement

28
00:02:25,321 --> 00:02:30,900
at Floyd Garrett's Muscle Car Museum, just outside Knoxville, Tennessee.

29
00:02:32,780 --> 00:02:37,500
This is Muscle Car Mecca, drawing thousands from across the country

30
00:02:37,501 --> 00:02:43,340
to drool over 90 of the cleanest, shiniest, fastest automobiles to ever hit the streets.

31
00:02:43,980 --> 00:02:46,700
And yes, they all run.

32
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:51,060
This engine is legendary.

33
00:02:51,880 --> 00:02:53,580
The Pontiac 389.

34
00:02:54,460 --> 00:02:59,080
Similar to the one that would be placed in 1964 Pontiac GTOs.

35
00:03:00,160 --> 00:03:04,880
Thundering chunks of steel that many consider the first real muscle cars.

36
00:03:06,220 --> 00:03:11,800
But museum owner Floyd Garrett thinks the roots of muscle car design go back to the mid-50s.

37
00:03:11,801 --> 00:03:16,360
with cars like the 1955 Chrysler 300.

38
00:03:18,200 --> 00:03:21,400
This is the famous hemispherical head engine,

39
00:03:21,680 --> 00:03:23,780
what they call the Chrysler Hemi engine,

40
00:03:24,140 --> 00:03:25,700
and it produced 300 horsepower,

41
00:03:25,940 --> 00:03:27,820
and that's where they started the 300 from.

42
00:03:28,520 --> 00:03:31,600
This car was really heavy for the NASCAR circuit,

43
00:03:31,940 --> 00:03:33,640
but you stretch it out on the long end,

44
00:03:33,641 --> 00:03:35,900
it would eat your peanut butter and jelly.

45
00:03:35,901 --> 00:03:46,240
When it came to style and speed, Chevrolet was king in the late 50s.

46
00:03:46,780 --> 00:03:53,100
The famous 57 Chevys moved off dealer lots as fast as salesmen could do the paperwork.

47
00:03:54,720 --> 00:04:02,040
But the 58s broke new ground with a 348 cubic inch engine, 315 horsepower,

48
00:04:02,041 --> 00:04:06,320
that would lead the way to future high-performance power plants.

49
00:04:08,360 --> 00:04:10,880
It was the first year for the big block engine,

50
00:04:11,360 --> 00:04:15,620
and this car is totally an unrestored car and a very rare car.

51
00:04:15,700 --> 00:04:17,760
It's one of fewer than 100 produced.

52
00:04:19,440 --> 00:04:22,720
58 Chevys also upped the ante with TriPower,

53
00:04:22,721 --> 00:04:29,020
three dual-barrel carburetors designed to suck in as much oxygen as possible

54
00:04:29,021 --> 00:04:31,760
to generate power and speed.

55
00:04:32,040 --> 00:04:39,660
With the big block engine and multiple carves, cars came off assembly lines in the early

56
00:04:39,661 --> 00:04:45,240
60s with muscle to burn.

57
00:04:45,241 --> 00:04:50,920
The race was on to see who could build the biggest engine with the most horsepower mounted

58
00:04:50,921 --> 00:04:54,600
in the lightest weight car.

59
00:04:54,601 --> 00:04:57,920
This is a 1961 Ford Starliner.

60
00:04:57,921 --> 00:05:01,880
This was the first year for the multiple carburation on a Ford engine.

61
00:05:02,500 --> 00:05:06,220
It came out with 375 horsepower with a single four-barrel.

62
00:05:08,200 --> 00:05:12,480
The 61 Ford boasted a 390 cubic inch engine.

63
00:05:13,560 --> 00:05:21,820
By 1963, Ford was producing 427 cubic inch motors with horsepower as high as 425.

64
00:05:21,821 --> 00:05:28,820
And the miraculous moment comes when the world's newest automobile springs to life under its own power.

65
00:05:31,820 --> 00:05:37,820
Chrysler entered the muscle car race with its own strong Mopar Motor Parts Division competitors.

66
00:05:38,820 --> 00:05:45,820
Ultimately, a Dodge with a 426 cubic inch power plant and 425 horsepower.

67
00:05:45,821 --> 00:05:54,060
In the early 60s, this was the ultimate factory drag car, period.

68
00:05:54,061 --> 00:05:56,880
Nothing could touch this when this came out.

69
00:05:56,881 --> 00:06:01,060
They all had dual quads, even the street hemis.

70
00:06:01,061 --> 00:06:05,360
Look, a motor is basically an air pump.

71
00:06:05,361 --> 00:06:10,860
Air goes in here, goes out there, and the faster you can move that air, the better the

72
00:06:10,861 --> 00:06:11,860
motor.

73
00:06:11,861 --> 00:06:16,760
So with the Hemi heads, you know, you've got the plug in the middle, you've got the intake valve, the exhaust valve.

74
00:06:17,520 --> 00:06:25,720
You know, from three to seven grand, the air is flowing so quickly and so perfectly through this motor that it was the perfect air pump.

75
00:06:26,280 --> 00:06:28,260
And which means it's a perfect muscle car motor.

76
00:06:30,860 --> 00:06:33,180
This is a pretty untouched car. It's a pretty big deal piece.

77
00:06:33,500 --> 00:06:34,200
You want to hear it?

78
00:06:41,860 --> 00:06:52,620
While all of these engine wars were being waged among Chevy, Ford and Chrysler, a quiet

79
00:06:52,621 --> 00:06:57,380
revolution was starting in the Pontiac division of General Motors.

80
00:06:57,381 --> 00:07:01,680
Bunky Knudsen took over Pontiac as General Manager.

81
00:07:01,681 --> 00:07:10,180
He hired Pete Estes as Chief Engineer and a promising young designer named John DeLorean.

82
00:07:10,181 --> 00:07:13,420
Their goal was to change Pontiac's image dramatically.

83
00:07:14,860 --> 00:07:19,740
The problem they had was they built a very old folks car.

84
00:07:20,640 --> 00:07:23,760
And as a consequence, their audience was dying off.

85
00:07:23,980 --> 00:07:26,060
So the business was diminishing.

86
00:07:26,380 --> 00:07:29,800
There was zero excitement because those people didn't want excitement.

87
00:07:30,680 --> 00:07:33,420
And it was a pretty sad situation.

88
00:07:35,300 --> 00:07:38,000
To get an image change off to a quick start,

89
00:07:38,001 --> 00:07:41,300
the trio of Knudsen, Estes, and DeLorean

90
00:07:41,301 --> 00:07:44,580
put together a couple high-performance engines

91
00:07:44,581 --> 00:07:48,040
that immediately caught the attention of muscle car buyers.

92
00:07:49,060 --> 00:07:50,200
Called Super Duties,

93
00:07:50,820 --> 00:07:56,500
they were available in 389 and 421 cubic inch configurations.

94
00:07:57,660 --> 00:08:01,420
This is a 63 Catalina with a 421 Super Duty.

95
00:08:02,280 --> 00:08:04,960
Unlike the 61 389 Super Duty,

96
00:08:04,961 --> 00:08:07,000
there's, of course, a bigger cubic inch displacement

97
00:08:07,001 --> 00:08:11,080
and had the two fours with the aluminum intake manifold.

98
00:08:11,660 --> 00:08:16,020
It was rated at a conservative 405 basic horsepower.

99
00:08:19,280 --> 00:08:24,800
In the early 60s, car makers who wanted to sell their super-duty high-performance streetcars

100
00:08:24,801 --> 00:08:28,780
knew they had to put modified versions on the racetrack.

101
00:08:30,780 --> 00:08:36,160
There was no better way to sell a car than to have it win in front of thousands of race fans.

102
00:08:37,000 --> 00:08:46,900
The phrase, win on Sunday, sell on Monday, became Detroit's mantra, and Pontiac's new super duties were winning big.

103
00:08:48,980 --> 00:09:01,260
I think one year we won 49 out of 53 stock car races in the country, so it was a thing to generate excitement for the division and to bring it into a younger audience.

104
00:09:01,261 --> 00:09:11,462
And as a consequence, we did lose all the old people, but we did wind up with a young, more affluent customer, which is exactly where you want to be in the car

105
00:09:11,463 --> 00:09:12,163
business.

106
00:09:13,780 --> 00:09:17,580
Pontiac also added a design change that impressed young buyers.

107
00:09:19,500 --> 00:09:27,320
I came up with the concept, just sort of accidentally, of widening the tread, so that led to the so-called wide-track Pontiac.

108
00:09:27,321 --> 00:09:33,160
I think we widened the tread about four inches and brought the wheels out to the sides of the car,

109
00:09:33,260 --> 00:09:36,740
and all of a sudden the car took a new, planted look.

110
00:09:36,820 --> 00:09:41,980
It looked like it belonged on a road, not like a guy with a hat on it was too big for him.

111
00:09:42,340 --> 00:09:47,300
It was incredible how that little thing made such a giant difference.

112
00:09:49,800 --> 00:09:55,580
By 1963, Pontiac had joined the muscle car world as a major player.

113
00:09:55,581 --> 00:10:00,700
But a decision by General Motors Management would shake up all of its divisions

114
00:10:00,701 --> 00:10:04,260
and have a major impact on muscle car design.

115
00:10:05,020 --> 00:10:11,580
When we return, the death of GM race cars and the birth of the legendary GTO.

116
00:10:17,080 --> 00:10:23,580
The first drag strip, the Santa Ana Drags, began running on an airfield in Southern California in 1950.

117
00:10:23,581 --> 00:10:28,000
It gained popularity because of its revolutionary speed clocks.

118
00:10:28,540 --> 00:10:30,320
Modern Marvels will return in a moment.

119
00:10:33,400 --> 00:10:38,100
In 1963, General Motors faced a unique dilemma.

120
00:10:38,980 --> 00:10:43,740
Their divisions of Chevrolet, Pontiac, and Oldsmobile were too successful.

121
00:10:45,640 --> 00:10:51,080
Pontiac ad agency rep Jim Wangers found himself trying to sell fewer cars.

122
00:10:51,081 --> 00:10:58,100
At that time they were getting the myriad of close to 55% of the market

123
00:10:58,101 --> 00:11:01,480
And the government thought if you got anywhere close to 60

124
00:11:01,481 --> 00:11:03,340
We're going to have to seriously look at you

125
00:11:03,341 --> 00:11:06,320
And figure out how we may break it up

126
00:11:06,321 --> 00:11:08,460
So one of the things GM then

127
00:11:08,461 --> 00:11:12,060
Not as an industry but as a corporate entity

128
00:11:12,061 --> 00:11:16,800
The corporation said we're going to pull out of any active participation in motorsports

129
00:11:16,801 --> 00:11:22,480
no longer able to sell his cars on Monday that won on Sunday.

130
00:11:23,500 --> 00:11:25,740
John DeLorean went back to the drawing board,

131
00:11:26,800 --> 00:11:30,240
assessing who bought which GM cars and why.

132
00:11:31,840 --> 00:11:36,100
The Oldsmobile guy was always the guy with the checkered pants

133
00:11:36,101 --> 00:11:40,020
and the white buck shoes and the Orlon sports jacket.

134
00:11:40,540 --> 00:11:44,540
The Buick guy was sort of a pot-bellied, semi-balding.

135
00:11:44,541 --> 00:11:46,880
I mean, these are the images you had in your mind.

136
00:11:47,020 --> 00:11:50,640
And then, of course, the Cadillac owner was the little gray-haired old lady.

137
00:11:50,860 --> 00:11:54,820
So you saw the person you wanted to appeal to.

138
00:11:54,900 --> 00:12:01,680
And what we were looking for is the young, good-looking guy with a good job and making a decent living who liked a little excitement.

139
00:12:03,760 --> 00:12:09,540
While other GM division managers conducted formal buyer surveys and poured over demographic statistics,

140
00:12:09,541 --> 00:12:10,349
statistics.

141
00:12:10,350 --> 00:12:16,820
John DeLorean spent his time researching what was happening in trend-setting California.

142
00:12:18,560 --> 00:12:25,520
He was always out there looking to get information to form an opinion about what was going on so that

143
00:12:25,521 --> 00:12:32,300
when he marketed, he marketed on target. It wasn't long before DeLorean's research

144
00:12:32,301 --> 00:12:38,880
resulted in a new prototype, but it didn't come through GM's official design approval process.

145
00:12:39,540 --> 00:12:42,540
DeLorean did it his way.

146
00:12:42,541 --> 00:12:48,540
I took a smaller car, a Tempest, and I had a big engine put in it, some aggressive tires.

147
00:12:48,541 --> 00:12:54,540
I bought a Hurst shifter out of a catalog somewhere, put a more aggressive suspension on it,

148
00:12:54,541 --> 00:12:59,540
and that was a car that I used driving back and forth to work.

149
00:12:59,541 --> 00:13:03,540
It was fun. And I'd lend it to somebody for the weekend.

150
00:13:03,541 --> 00:13:06,540
I'd say, here, you ought to drive this, and I could never get it back.

151
00:13:06,541 --> 00:13:10,929
back. So I said, well, I think there's a market there.

152
00:13:10,930 --> 00:13:14,180
And indeed, it proved to be true. It was

153
00:13:14,181 --> 00:13:17,660
just a car that everybody fell in love with the minute they got into it.

154
00:13:19,560 --> 00:13:28,260
DeLorean decided to call his new car the GTO, Gran Turismo Omolegato, a class of car designated

155
00:13:28,261 --> 00:13:32,440
by the Federation of International Automobiles, headquartered in France.

156
00:13:35,580 --> 00:13:40,960
At the time, the only other car designated GTO was the Ferrari.

157
00:13:43,160 --> 00:13:45,920
It really was a tribute to Ferrari.

158
00:13:46,240 --> 00:13:47,420
We weren't trying to copy it.

159
00:13:47,440 --> 00:13:51,060
It was a tribute to them because they were clearly, and they still are,

160
00:13:51,160 --> 00:13:52,920
you know, the best of the best.

161
00:13:52,921 --> 00:13:59,540
Jim Wangers was one of the chosen few to drive the prototype GTO

162
00:13:59,541 --> 00:14:04,060
This thing was just drunk with torque

163
00:14:04,061 --> 00:14:07,420
All you had to do was just stab it a little bit

164
00:14:07,421 --> 00:14:09,420
And you had instant tire smoke

165
00:14:09,421 --> 00:14:13,540
And instant rear end squeal and howl

166
00:14:13,541 --> 00:14:17,420
And all of the things that really were a major contributor

167
00:14:17,421 --> 00:14:19,440
To the image of what performance

168
00:14:19,441 --> 00:14:22,640
And what automotive excitement was all about

169
00:14:22,641 --> 00:14:29,640
Everybody was impressed with the GTO, except General Motors Management.

170
00:14:29,641 --> 00:14:35,640
Suddenly, their flagship line, Chevrolet, had unexpected competition from within.

171
00:14:35,641 --> 00:14:45,640
Plus, the GTO violated a GM policy, allowing big, powerful engines to be placed only in expensive, full-size cars.

172
00:14:45,641 --> 00:14:48,920
size cars.

173
00:14:48,921 --> 00:14:50,880
DeLorean got around this restriction

174
00:14:50,881 --> 00:14:54,640
by offering the first GTOs not as specific models,

175
00:14:54,641 --> 00:14:58,940
but as high-end options on the midsize Pontiac Tempest.

176
00:14:58,941 --> 00:15:03,520
GM management told DeLorean that 1964 GTO production

177
00:15:03,521 --> 00:15:07,320
would be limited to a maximum of 5,000 cars, which

178
00:15:07,321 --> 00:15:10,660
had to be specially ordered by dealers.

179
00:15:10,661 --> 00:15:14,580
DeLorean and Wangers coaxed, cajoled, and begged dealers

180
00:15:14,581 --> 00:15:16,620
to order at least one of the GTOs.

181
00:15:17,360 --> 00:15:21,280
Within weeks, the cars rolled off delivery trucks across the country,

182
00:15:21,660 --> 00:15:24,060
and eager salesmen took them for a spin.

183
00:15:26,900 --> 00:15:28,720
These guys would come back, and they'd say,

184
00:15:28,800 --> 00:15:31,620
holy smoke, what have we got here?

185
00:15:31,940 --> 00:15:35,440
And they'd run right in to the sales manager or the dealer himself

186
00:15:35,441 --> 00:15:37,400
and say, hey, order 10 more of these,

187
00:15:37,401 --> 00:15:41,680
because this is going to walk off our showroom floor, let me tell you.

188
00:15:42,640 --> 00:15:43,440
And they did.

189
00:15:43,441 --> 00:15:54,880
And so in a matter of less than a month after the cars began to show up actually in the showrooms, we had over 15,000 additional orders.

190
00:15:55,960 --> 00:15:59,280
For GM executives, there was no turning back.

191
00:15:59,920 --> 00:16:02,540
Like it or not, they had a success on their hands.

192
00:16:03,020 --> 00:16:07,660
And the hottest GM car was no longer a Chevy, but a Pontiac.

193
00:16:07,661 --> 00:16:14,960
By then, the egg had been broken and the omelet was made, so it really didn't make an awful lot of difference.

194
00:16:15,620 --> 00:16:20,980
See, then you had the market driving the product instead of us driving the product into the market.

195
00:16:21,340 --> 00:16:23,920
The public were demanding more cars than we could build.

196
00:16:25,640 --> 00:16:33,300
Ironically, the decision to take General Motors cars out of professional racing was the best thing that could have happened to Pontiac.

197
00:16:33,301 --> 00:16:40,300
It forced creative engineers to take power reserved for the track and put it on the street.

198
00:16:40,301 --> 00:16:50,300
See, the problem you had was that the guys that drove exciting cars were the chubby guys on Wall Street who didn't know how to drive.

199
00:16:50,301 --> 00:16:58,300
And the young people who really knew how to do it and got a shot out of it, got a high, couldn't afford them.

200
00:16:58,301 --> 00:16:59,066
couldn't afford them.

201
00:16:59,067 --> 00:17:01,800
Well, all of a sudden, you were putting a car in their hands that would run

202
00:17:01,801 --> 00:17:09,172
with it. You know, about any car in the world at a very low price.

203
00:17:09,173 --> 00:17:12,300
The Pontiac GTO, priced less

204
00:17:12,301 --> 00:17:17,369
than $3,000, would literally establish a new American subculture.

205
00:17:17,370 --> 00:17:19,240
It was a production car.

206
00:17:19,340 --> 00:17:22,487
It just had a really good horsepower to weight ratio.

207
00:17:22,488 --> 00:17:24,980
And with a light rear end, you could light

208
00:17:24,981 --> 00:17:30,000
the tires up and the tires in those days weren't as sticky as they are today nor were they as wide

209
00:17:30,001 --> 00:17:34,960
so you know you could impress your friends pretty easy you know and square around the corner and

210
00:17:34,961 --> 00:17:41,560
squeal the tires you know they were just good streetable cars they just happened to be fast

211
00:17:41,561 --> 00:17:47,820
and they were affordable street power was now in the hands of working men and women

212
00:17:47,821 --> 00:17:53,140
young drivers would spend endless hours tinkering under the hoods of their dream cars

213
00:17:53,141 --> 00:17:57,560
and cruising for a chance to show off their speed.

214
00:17:59,280 --> 00:18:04,460
When we return, the engineering and marketing savvy behind the GTO.

215
00:18:06,240 --> 00:18:11,700
And Chevrolet enters the muscle car game with the Chevelle SS Supersport.

216
00:18:15,200 --> 00:18:18,200
Ronda Hartman-Smith is the world's fastest mom.

217
00:18:18,780 --> 00:18:23,300
Rhonda is ranked number 10 in championship point standing of the National Hot Rod Association.

218
00:18:24,060 --> 00:18:29,420
She took the seat of her family-owned dragster in 1993 after her husband was injured in a race.

219
00:18:29,880 --> 00:18:31,620
Muscle Cars will return in a moment.

220
00:18:37,440 --> 00:18:40,540
At Floyd Garrett's Muscle Car Museum in Tennessee,

221
00:18:41,240 --> 00:18:46,660
only one car is displayed with its body stripped away to reveal the mechanics underneath.

222
00:18:46,661 --> 00:18:49,660
It's a Pontiac GTO.

223
00:18:49,661 --> 00:18:56,660
Well, this is a good example of an early GTO chassis engine drivetrain.

224
00:18:56,661 --> 00:18:59,660
Good depiction from the years of 64 to 67.

225
00:18:59,661 --> 00:19:06,660
The engine is a 389 with a standard four-barrel, and the 389 being its last year in 1966.

226
00:19:06,661 --> 00:19:11,660
There were some slight differences between 64 and 65 with the head changes.

227
00:19:11,661 --> 00:19:15,660
And there was also some camming changes, which would give it about 10 more horsepower.

228
00:19:15,661 --> 00:19:22,980
It has a standard Muncie four-speed with the Hurst shifter, which the GTO is famous for, along with its white marble knob.

229
00:19:24,560 --> 00:19:32,160
When designing the GTO, John DeLorean understood that a performance car was only as good as its manual gear shifter.

230
00:19:35,540 --> 00:19:39,640
He knew that standard GM shifters were not designed for racing.

231
00:19:39,641 --> 00:19:47,980
Again, bucking the desires of top GM management, he succeeded in placing a far superior shifter

232
00:19:47,981 --> 00:19:53,080
from an outside manufacturer in every GTO.

233
00:19:53,081 --> 00:19:58,720
We have the two shifters here as examples of factory production and an aftermarket production

234
00:19:58,721 --> 00:20:02,320
unit produced by the George Hurst Company.

235
00:20:02,321 --> 00:20:07,520
The factory shifter would wear out easily and it had no stops to keep you from pushing

236
00:20:07,521 --> 00:20:10,597
this shifter literally through the dashboard.

237
00:20:10,598 --> 00:20:13,400
The Hertz shifter, to the contrary, has a

238
00:20:13,401 --> 00:20:19,760
full perimeter box around the shift levers, and it very specifically has stops, which

239
00:20:19,761 --> 00:20:25,640
are adjustable, to allow a very positive, hard shift, time after time after time.

240
00:20:25,641 --> 00:20:38,760
Following their debut in 1964, the annual increase in sales of GTOs was staggering.

241
00:20:39,760 --> 00:20:43,120
Model year 1964 sold more than 30,000.

242
00:20:44,300 --> 00:20:45,920
It happens every time.

243
00:20:46,580 --> 00:20:53,060
A guy goes out and gets a genuine Tiger, a Pontiac GTO that is, and guess who suddenly

244
00:20:53,061 --> 00:20:54,280
loves cats?

245
00:20:54,281 --> 00:21:00,780
In 1965, Pontiac adopted a tiger campaign for the GTO,

246
00:21:01,040 --> 00:21:02,780
with catchphrases like,

247
00:21:03,320 --> 00:21:08,080
for the man who wouldn't mind racing a tiger if someone would only put wheels on it.

248
00:21:10,380 --> 00:21:18,040
87,000 GTOs were sold in 1965, and 96,000 in 1966.

249
00:21:20,280 --> 00:21:22,980
When you pull into a gas station, for example,

250
00:21:22,981 --> 00:21:28,180
and you know he said hey check my oil with you gas station attendant in those days you didn't

251
00:21:28,181 --> 00:21:33,540
pump your own but come over and raise the hood and then all of a sudden holy smoke look at that

252
00:21:34,020 --> 00:21:37,780
and if there was a little crowd around in the gas station they all come over and say

253
00:21:37,781 --> 00:21:44,180
holy smoke that looked good it's that powerful three carburetor that was part of the job and

254
00:21:44,181 --> 00:21:45,420
and part of the sales.

255
00:21:49,600 --> 00:21:51,240
The Tiger scores again.

256
00:21:52,900 --> 00:21:57,900
Pontiac GTO for 66, 6.5 liters of young Tiger.

257
00:21:59,700 --> 00:22:03,120
To what did the GTO owe such sudden loyalty and fame?

258
00:22:04,140 --> 00:22:07,460
First and foremost, it was one hell of a car.

259
00:22:07,461 --> 00:22:10,800
Extremely powerful and beautifully styled.

260
00:22:10,801 --> 00:22:12,880
Some design elements, however,

261
00:22:12,881 --> 00:22:15,340
may have looked like they were added for higher performance.

262
00:22:16,100 --> 00:22:18,240
In reality, they were not.

263
00:22:20,540 --> 00:22:24,240
One of the things that has been a tremendous misconception over the years

264
00:22:24,241 --> 00:22:29,040
is that this does not produce any ramming effect.

265
00:22:29,041 --> 00:22:33,260
It is not a true ram air type of assembly.

266
00:22:33,420 --> 00:22:37,040
In fact, other than a styling pleasantry,

267
00:22:37,740 --> 00:22:42,700
this is absolutely the stupidest place to put a hood scoop on this entire hood.

268
00:22:42,701 --> 00:22:44,280
and Pontiac knew it at the time.

269
00:22:44,380 --> 00:22:45,840
They did it basically for styling.

270
00:22:46,380 --> 00:22:49,400
And the reason why that is so stupid is because the airflow

271
00:22:49,401 --> 00:22:54,200
comes off the front of the car over this nicely shaped hood.

272
00:22:54,860 --> 00:22:58,920
And the faster you go, the more the air goes up higher.

273
00:22:59,400 --> 00:23:02,460
So when you get at speeds 45 to 50 miles an hour,

274
00:23:02,520 --> 00:23:03,980
this is absolutely a vacuum.

275
00:23:04,880 --> 00:23:07,940
You're not getting anything in there but cooler temperatures.

276
00:23:07,941 --> 00:23:10,720
You're getting no airflow whatsoever.

277
00:23:10,721 --> 00:23:17,380
For most GTO buyers, the fact that a hood scoop was non-functional was irrelevant.

278
00:23:18,180 --> 00:23:19,680
It looked really cool.

279
00:23:20,560 --> 00:23:26,400
Engineering designers recognized the value of image, and so did the GTO marketing team.

280
00:23:31,340 --> 00:23:34,660
With the dramatic success of the Pontiac GTO,

281
00:23:34,661 --> 00:23:41,780
managers of the General Motors Chevrolet division realized they too needed to attract the younger car buyer.

282
00:23:42,880 --> 00:23:47,460
In 1967, the Chevelle SS Supersport hit the mark,

283
00:23:47,880 --> 00:23:58,060
with V8 engines boasting 396 cubic inches and horsepower options of 325, 350, and even 375.

284
00:23:58,061 --> 00:24:05,060
The 67 SS put Chevrolet on the muscle car map.

285
00:24:05,061 --> 00:24:09,060
63,000 Chevelles were sold in 1967.

286
00:24:09,061 --> 00:24:14,060
Chevrolet, blue collar power.

287
00:24:14,061 --> 00:24:16,060
Lots of power.

288
00:24:16,061 --> 00:24:23,060
The Chevelle was a good looking intermediate body that could take the biggest engine that General Motors could build.

289
00:24:23,061 --> 00:24:27,820
and they had stout transmissions, good robust drive lines,

290
00:24:28,460 --> 00:24:29,860
it could handle a real thrashing.

291
00:24:30,800 --> 00:24:34,060
The handling, again, and the braking was a little on the marginal side,

292
00:24:34,460 --> 00:24:38,360
but in a stoplight drag race with a properly set up Chevelle,

293
00:24:38,900 --> 00:24:41,640
it could give any other muscle car a real run for its money.

294
00:24:42,960 --> 00:24:47,620
The General Motors Oldsmobile division also wanted into the Muscle Car Act,

295
00:24:48,220 --> 00:24:50,460
and so was born the 442.

296
00:24:50,461 --> 00:24:59,900
To distinguish itself from the GTO and Chevelle, Oldsmobile emphasized better balance in engineering and its muscle car.

297
00:25:00,660 --> 00:25:05,480
Unfortunately, that meant more weight and consequently less performance.

298
00:25:07,860 --> 00:25:11,600
That's the demon of any race car, whether it's on the street or the strip.

299
00:25:12,220 --> 00:25:13,160
Weight is the enemy.

300
00:25:13,800 --> 00:25:19,540
Though the 442 had a little bit more of a luxurious bend to it than the GTO.

301
00:25:19,541 --> 00:25:21,540
So that was the Oldsmobile tradition.

302
00:25:21,900 --> 00:25:25,340
Put a little bit of luxury in there as well as brute power.

303
00:25:26,960 --> 00:25:29,280
Try as they might to compete with the GTO,

304
00:25:30,020 --> 00:25:31,500
when it came to muscle cars,

305
00:25:31,880 --> 00:25:35,660
the other GM divisions were continually playing catch-up to Pontiac

306
00:25:35,661 --> 00:25:38,460
in both performance and design.

307
00:25:40,840 --> 00:25:43,100
If you look at most Pontiac cars,

308
00:25:44,020 --> 00:25:47,100
going back to the 64 Grand Prix cars,

309
00:25:47,280 --> 00:25:48,640
look at the interior appointments,

310
00:25:48,641 --> 00:25:51,800
look at the body appointments, look at the style wheels.

311
00:25:52,400 --> 00:25:56,860
They're always one step ahead of the rest of the GM lines.

312
00:25:57,740 --> 00:26:01,900
The other lines had their design engineers, which were very strong people,

313
00:26:02,320 --> 00:26:05,180
but they tended to walk the straight and narrow line,

314
00:26:05,640 --> 00:26:08,960
and John DeLorean didn't know such thing.

315
00:26:12,840 --> 00:26:16,900
With glowing reviews and the youthful image of a Tiger ad campaign,

316
00:26:16,901 --> 00:26:20,100
the GTO remained supreme.

317
00:26:22,580 --> 00:26:28,040
Even when a GM chairman decided Tigers were not fitting for the company's reputation.

318
00:26:29,180 --> 00:26:32,760
He snapped, so to speak. He finally said, this is enough.

319
00:26:33,180 --> 00:26:35,100
I want this stopped.

320
00:26:35,280 --> 00:26:40,540
This is not an appropriate way for a General Motors division to portray itself.

321
00:26:41,580 --> 00:26:46,540
Pontiac changed its GTO Tiger image to a more sophisticated one.

322
00:26:46,900 --> 00:26:49,000
Based on three simple words.

323
00:26:49,540 --> 00:26:53,260
This is the great one, the ultimate driving machine.

324
00:26:53,880 --> 00:26:57,020
And if you don't know what that means, you're excused.

325
00:26:57,200 --> 00:27:01,500
We took the letters GTO and turned them around a little bit,

326
00:27:01,560 --> 00:27:05,500
and we called the car the great one, or was TGO.

327
00:27:06,340 --> 00:27:08,920
And fortunately, it was successful. It worked.

328
00:27:09,120 --> 00:27:11,700
You know, the market realized we weren't being illiterate,

329
00:27:11,840 --> 00:27:13,800
but it was, in fact, the great one.

330
00:27:13,801 --> 00:27:16,660
And so the GTO then became known as the great one.

331
00:27:16,900 --> 00:27:21,740
The great one is bucket seats, carpeting, and a walnut-styled instrument panel.

332
00:27:22,740 --> 00:27:25,700
The great one is Pontiac GTO for 1967.

333
00:27:26,820 --> 00:27:29,500
Isn't it time you decided to ride the wide track winning streak?

334
00:27:31,100 --> 00:27:36,200
Ride the wide track winning streak.

335
00:27:37,260 --> 00:27:40,640
Ride the wide, wide, wide track Pontiac.

336
00:27:42,640 --> 00:27:45,800
Just as the power of the GTO appealed to men,

337
00:27:45,801 --> 00:27:48,040
Its style appealed to women.

338
00:27:49,020 --> 00:27:53,880
No woman minded being seen behind the wheel of anything known as the Great One.

339
00:27:54,800 --> 00:27:58,200
But just as the sophisticated new ad campaign caught on,

340
00:27:58,920 --> 00:28:02,060
car buyers themselves came up with their own nickname.

341
00:28:03,020 --> 00:28:05,520
The young people who had these cars, you know,

342
00:28:05,600 --> 00:28:09,860
they felt that what's the closest word that the letters GTO spell?

343
00:28:10,680 --> 00:28:12,100
And that was GOAT.

344
00:28:12,101 --> 00:28:17,440
For some reason, it came out of the market, the owners themselves,

345
00:28:17,600 --> 00:28:20,160
and they began to nickname the car, the GOAT.

346
00:28:20,480 --> 00:28:25,140
And it's even as popular today as it was when it first came into its own.

347
00:28:25,340 --> 00:28:26,520
A GTO is a GOAT.

348
00:28:27,060 --> 00:28:32,540
Call it what you will, a GTO, a Tiger, the Great One, or even a GOAT.

349
00:28:33,120 --> 00:28:37,560
The Pontiac GTO became the muscle car to catch in the 1960s.

350
00:28:37,561 --> 00:28:45,760
When we return, the race is on to catch the goat.

351
00:28:49,140 --> 00:28:52,180
Theories of where the term drag racing began are varied.

352
00:28:52,840 --> 00:28:55,280
Explanations range from a geographical location.

353
00:28:55,720 --> 00:28:58,400
A drag was a city's main street to mechanical.

354
00:28:58,920 --> 00:29:02,340
To drag the gears meant to hold the transmission gear longer than normal.

355
00:29:02,880 --> 00:29:04,680
Model Marvels will return in a moment.

356
00:29:07,560 --> 00:29:16,760
As engines roared at stoplights across the country during the late 1960s, no car manufacturer

357
00:29:16,761 --> 00:29:20,680
gave Pontiac better competition than Chrysler.

358
00:29:20,681 --> 00:29:25,920
Mopar put out a line of performance add-ons that had muscle car owners spending endless

359
00:29:25,921 --> 00:29:32,400
hours in their garages, getting every last bit of power and speed out of already awesome

360
00:29:32,401 --> 00:29:34,880
power plants under the hood.

361
00:29:35,840 --> 00:29:42,560
It may have taken the Chrysler lines of Dodge and Plymouth a few years to figure out what to put up against the GTO.

362
00:29:43,260 --> 00:29:49,820
But when they finally placed their own mid-sized muscle cars on the street, they were a force to be reckoned with.

363
00:29:50,620 --> 00:29:52,720
Sheriff, I bought that car cash money.

364
00:29:53,200 --> 00:29:55,400
You can't afford no car like this.

365
00:29:55,960 --> 00:29:57,920
These fancy Italian jobbies cost a fortune.

366
00:29:58,180 --> 00:30:00,220
New Dodge Challenger coupe, Buford.

367
00:30:00,500 --> 00:30:01,200
Challenger?

368
00:30:01,280 --> 00:30:02,860
And it don't cost no fortune.

369
00:30:02,861 --> 00:30:04,580
Look at the way this baby's built.

370
00:30:04,880 --> 00:30:08,060
My Jeep, we've got torsion bars.

371
00:30:09,020 --> 00:30:11,260
Taking a cue from the Pontiac GTO,

372
00:30:12,240 --> 00:30:14,820
Dodges and Plymouths stressed price and performance

373
00:30:14,821 --> 00:30:19,160
with optional Hemi heads and carburetors in six-packs.

374
00:30:20,540 --> 00:30:21,960
It's just six barrels.

375
00:30:22,160 --> 00:30:25,600
It's three Holley two-barrels in line.

376
00:30:27,120 --> 00:30:29,540
You're basically running off the middle carb

377
00:30:29,541 --> 00:30:32,460
until you start putting your foot in it,

378
00:30:32,461 --> 00:30:35,380
and then the outer four come into play.

379
00:30:35,640 --> 00:30:39,840
And if you really put your foot in it and those outer four just slam open,

380
00:30:40,680 --> 00:30:43,440
you better be holding on because, I mean, it's just violent.

381
00:30:51,480 --> 00:30:56,420
For those muscle car lovers who didn't want to deal with the complexity of hemi engines,

382
00:30:57,660 --> 00:31:00,660
Chrysler had another power player waiting in the wings.

383
00:31:01,460 --> 00:31:03,020
The Plymouth Roadrunner.

384
00:31:04,300 --> 00:31:07,800
This is a 69 1⁄2 Roadrunner, 446-pack.

385
00:31:08,360 --> 00:31:13,900
And on the street, these engines were rated at 390 horsepower, which a Hemi was 425.

386
00:31:14,700 --> 00:31:20,980
But unless you were one of the factory teams or somebody that could really tune up a Hemi, which was temperamental,

387
00:31:21,500 --> 00:31:24,840
these cars would pretty much eat a Hemi's lunch on the street.

388
00:31:30,660 --> 00:31:35,500
What is that Fairlane feeling?

389
00:31:36,280 --> 00:31:37,300
And what about Ford?

390
00:31:37,960 --> 00:31:40,140
What did it produce to compete with the GTO?

391
00:31:40,680 --> 00:31:43,660
Some describe it as a noticeable quickening of the pulse.

392
00:31:43,661 --> 00:31:49,740
The powerful Fairlane GTAs and Torinos were adapted from the racetrack for street use.

393
00:31:51,020 --> 00:31:57,440
Chuck Folger was a Ford engineer at the time when Fairlanes and GTOs raced head-to-head on the street.

394
00:31:58,300 --> 00:32:02,880
We were very, very successful on the racetracks and the drag strips.

395
00:32:03,420 --> 00:32:09,240
We were a little bit, I think, slow in catching up with competition on the street.

396
00:32:09,800 --> 00:32:13,520
That was partially due to the availability of parts

397
00:32:13,521 --> 00:32:19,040
and getting the philosophy to where you could develop the car

398
00:32:19,041 --> 00:32:20,820
and run it down the production line.

399
00:32:22,220 --> 00:32:25,820
Ultimately, Ford chose to compete for the young buyer

400
00:32:25,821 --> 00:32:30,860
with less powerful, but very stylish, pony cars.

401
00:32:31,900 --> 00:32:37,180
For drivers willing to sacrifice torque and speed for an eye-catching new design,

402
00:32:37,920 --> 00:32:40,000
Ford Mustangs filled the bill.

403
00:32:45,360 --> 00:32:49,220
Stock models couldn't come close to keeping up with the GTOs.

404
00:32:49,420 --> 00:32:54,340
In fact, very few Mustangs could really be considered performance cars.

405
00:32:55,100 --> 00:32:59,880
Those were modified by famous race car driver turned engineer, Carol Shelby.

406
00:33:00,920 --> 00:33:08,080
This is a 68 Shelby KR convertible.

407
00:33:09,680 --> 00:33:11,400
The KR is king of the road.

408
00:33:12,280 --> 00:33:13,860
They came out April 1st, 68.

409
00:33:14,280 --> 00:33:17,820
It correlated with the 428 Cobra Jet announcement.

410
00:33:18,540 --> 00:33:22,640
I don't consider many Mustangs to be muscle cars, but this just happens to be one of them.

411
00:33:22,641 --> 00:33:26,200
this car is a complete and total muscle car.

412
00:33:26,800 --> 00:33:28,120
You put headers on this thing,

413
00:33:28,780 --> 00:33:30,360
and she will keep up with anything.

414
00:33:34,620 --> 00:33:36,860
As the 60s came to a close,

415
00:33:37,380 --> 00:33:40,860
Pontiac's John DeLorean had one final trick up his sleeve

416
00:33:40,861 --> 00:33:44,120
to keep the GTO feeling fresh and powerful.

417
00:33:45,340 --> 00:33:49,740
In 1969, a new version rolled off the assembly line.

418
00:33:50,400 --> 00:33:52,520
DeLorean named it The Judge.

419
00:33:52,640 --> 00:33:57,840
The name really came through, there was a show on TV called Laugh-In.

420
00:33:58,440 --> 00:34:01,820
And one of the things that they had in every show was,

421
00:34:02,240 --> 00:34:05,480
sometimes Sammy Davis, sometimes somebody else.

422
00:34:06,280 --> 00:34:08,440
It would always end with, here comes the judge.

423
00:34:08,500 --> 00:34:09,860
And I thought that was sort of humorous.

424
00:34:09,861 --> 00:34:13,580
So I decided, well, we'll just call the car the judge,

425
00:34:13,680 --> 00:34:15,540
so people can say, here comes the judge.

426
00:34:22,640 --> 00:34:31,020
Although the Judge was available in a variety of colors, its signature was Carousel Red,

427
00:34:31,021 --> 00:34:34,360
a very bright reddish orange.

428
00:34:34,361 --> 00:34:41,140
This GTO was definitely a high-profile car, designed to be noticed.

429
00:34:41,141 --> 00:34:47,520
In true Pontiac form, however, the Judge continued to contain new innovations.

430
00:34:47,521 --> 00:34:52,560
end models boasted a redesigned form of ram air that could pump up performance.

431
00:34:54,080 --> 00:34:59,360
Ram air was basically put an opening in the front of the car, it could be either in the hood or in

432
00:34:59,361 --> 00:35:05,240
the grill, in which the air pressure generated by the speed of the car would tend to supercharge

433
00:35:05,241 --> 00:35:07,500
the engine, and that's what the ram air was.

434
00:35:07,501 --> 00:35:09,760
And it gave a significant increase in power,

435
00:35:10,160 --> 00:35:12,556
but only at extremely high speeds.

436
00:35:12,557 --> 00:35:16,220
The judge would indeed mark the beginning of the end

437
00:35:16,221 --> 00:35:17,640
of the muscle car era.

438
00:35:18,780 --> 00:35:20,120
When we return,

439
00:35:20,540 --> 00:35:21,900
why high-performance cars

440
00:35:21,901 --> 00:35:23,140
stopped being made

441
00:35:23,141 --> 00:35:26,020
and how modern muscle car lovers

442
00:35:26,021 --> 00:35:29,000
continue to keep the dream alive.

443
00:35:37,180 --> 00:35:39,480
In the lobby of Floyd Garrett's

444
00:35:39,481 --> 00:35:41,240
Muscle Car Museum in Tennessee,

445
00:35:41,980 --> 00:35:43,440
a bright red muscle car

446
00:35:43,441 --> 00:35:45,600
draws the attention of every visitor.

447
00:35:46,220 --> 00:35:51,860
For Floyd, it represents both a high point and the end of the muscle car era.

448
00:35:52,580 --> 00:35:56,860
This is a 1970 Chevelle SS454.

449
00:35:57,240 --> 00:35:59,940
It was the highest horsepower that had ever come out of Detroit.

450
00:36:00,360 --> 00:36:04,040
It's 454 cubic inches, 450 horsepower.

451
00:36:04,940 --> 00:36:11,000
And it's got the cowl inducted hood, the cold air package like you see on ram airs and shaker hoods on Mopars.

452
00:36:11,120 --> 00:36:15,800
This car is unusual because it's got an automatic transmission and it's got rear interior.

453
00:36:16,220 --> 00:36:20,660
This is as good as it ever got for Chevrolets in the muscle car era to me.

454
00:36:23,060 --> 00:36:27,500
Shortly after the last 1970 Chevelles rolled off the assembly line,

455
00:36:28,500 --> 00:36:30,940
muscle cars began declining in popularity.

456
00:36:31,960 --> 00:36:34,900
The first sign of change came from insurance companies.

457
00:36:38,500 --> 00:36:41,800
The insurance started to escalate, and pretty rapidly.

458
00:36:41,801 --> 00:36:50,260
They were cars that seemed to have a higher rate of accidents and a higher degree of damage than ordinary cars.

459
00:36:52,540 --> 00:36:59,500
The gasoline shortage of the early 1970s suddenly made gas-guzzling muscle cars less appealing.

460
00:37:00,680 --> 00:37:06,360
Government regulations to eliminate leaded gasoline and reduce emissions further affected the market.

461
00:37:07,220 --> 00:37:10,740
As years passed, high-performance engines were still available,

462
00:37:11,380 --> 00:37:14,660
but they were no longer within reach of a working man's budget.

463
00:37:15,660 --> 00:37:21,340
The difference was that the GTO was priced in a level that, you know,

464
00:37:21,420 --> 00:37:25,460
if a daddy wanted to do something nice for his son or daughter,

465
00:37:25,540 --> 00:37:30,100
he could buy him one to go to college with or for his last year of high school or whatever,

466
00:37:30,240 --> 00:37:31,480
because it wasn't a lot of money.

467
00:37:31,481 --> 00:37:39,380
By the early 1970s, the only reasonably priced muscle cars were those sold unused car lots.

468
00:37:43,880 --> 00:37:47,740
Those fortunate to have purchased and restored one of these bargains

469
00:37:47,741 --> 00:37:52,860
now have cars worth anywhere from $20,000 to $100,000.

470
00:37:53,640 --> 00:37:57,060
Those who sold their muscle cars for peanuts years ago

471
00:37:57,061 --> 00:37:59,200
not only missed out on the money,

472
00:37:59,980 --> 00:38:03,400
but the satisfaction of having a car they can actually fix.

473
00:38:05,280 --> 00:38:06,440
I do miss the era.

474
00:38:06,960 --> 00:38:10,380
I miss the ability to pop the hood and pull the engine apart,

475
00:38:10,560 --> 00:38:12,060
put it back together and turn the key,

476
00:38:12,180 --> 00:38:14,040
and it runs better than when I started.

477
00:38:14,840 --> 00:38:16,920
I miss not being able to do that with a new car.

478
00:38:19,680 --> 00:38:23,260
Happily, however, for many true lovers of vintage muscle cars,

479
00:38:23,700 --> 00:38:25,040
the era has not ended.

480
00:38:25,041 --> 00:38:32,040
Drag strips across the country regularly hold events, allowing anyone with a mind to race,

481
00:38:33,040 --> 00:38:35,040
to fly down a quarter-mile track.

482
00:38:35,041 --> 00:38:41,040
Street racing may be illegal, but there are many opportunities to test a car's performance

483
00:38:41,041 --> 00:38:44,040
in a safe, controlled environment.

484
00:38:49,040 --> 00:38:54,040
More significantly, the sport of performance racing seems to have attracted new generations.

485
00:38:55,040 --> 00:38:59,200
of young people, many prompted by their parents.

486
00:39:00,260 --> 00:39:01,260
It's a family thing.

487
00:39:01,360 --> 00:39:04,880
We actually got into it to keep my son out of trouble,

488
00:39:05,180 --> 00:39:06,920
and we do all of our own work.

489
00:39:07,020 --> 00:39:10,020
We totally restore our own cars, build our own engines.

490
00:39:10,600 --> 00:39:14,820
It was a big thrill, you know, going to school in my 66 Barracuda and stuff.

491
00:39:14,821 --> 00:39:18,320
You know, all the kids, you know, like, dang, you know, that's a nice car, you know.

492
00:39:18,640 --> 00:39:19,980
It was a real thrill out of it.

493
00:39:20,040 --> 00:39:21,400
I got, you know, it was fun.

494
00:39:21,900 --> 00:39:24,940
Picked up the car, had everything original in it, took it home, cleaned it up.

495
00:39:25,040 --> 00:39:28,140
Dropped a new motor, transmission, differential, everything's brand new.

496
00:39:28,780 --> 00:39:30,680
Threw an airbag suspension setup on it.

497
00:39:31,340 --> 00:39:33,160
And it's been running ever since.

498
00:39:33,720 --> 00:39:37,880
I just love automobiles, so I like to go fast too.

499
00:39:38,540 --> 00:39:41,240
So I'm getting ready to do some upgrades on it and I want to go faster.

500
00:39:41,480 --> 00:39:47,940
Hopefully I don't end up hurting the car or wrecking it or doing something to it that it won't be around for another 20 years.

501
00:39:47,941 --> 00:39:56,140
What continues to draw thousands of street racers every weekend to test their mettle?

502
00:39:57,140 --> 00:40:00,200
It may be the chance to race for the very first time

503
00:40:00,201 --> 00:40:05,260
or to take on a friendly adversary who looks unbeatable.

504
00:40:09,420 --> 00:40:12,820
Of course, the price of winning may be popping a head gasket

505
00:40:12,821 --> 00:40:15,800
or blowing a piston.

506
00:40:16,580 --> 00:40:17,460
Oh, you broke a rod?

507
00:40:17,940 --> 00:40:18,980
I think it's a broken crank.

508
00:40:20,980 --> 00:40:25,340
Many come to the drag strip simply because they still love the sounds.

509
00:40:28,360 --> 00:40:30,900
The sound's an important part of what you do.

510
00:40:31,480 --> 00:40:35,440
I bought a record that somebody made way back then called The Sound of Speed.

511
00:40:35,980 --> 00:40:37,900
It was nothing but engine noise.

512
00:40:39,760 --> 00:40:43,320
I put it on my hi-fi and listened to it by the hour.

513
00:40:43,321 --> 00:40:45,360
Man, it was so thrilling just to hear it.

514
00:40:45,361 --> 00:40:54,740
To hear these powerful engines just bellowing out vast amounts of unburnt hydrocarbons,

515
00:40:55,420 --> 00:41:00,500
the tires just, just scrabbling for traction,

516
00:41:01,400 --> 00:41:05,080
the exhaust notes were just music.

517
00:41:05,640 --> 00:41:09,420
To anyone that grew up in that era, that's the sound of our childhood.

518
00:41:15,360 --> 00:41:24,020
John DeLorean still gets nostalgic when he watches his GTOs race.

519
00:41:24,720 --> 00:41:32,300
They remind him of a time when nothing was more important than creating objects of desire, fun, and excitement.

520
00:41:33,000 --> 00:41:34,620
Affordable to the working man.

521
00:41:37,100 --> 00:41:40,700
In those days, it was just so thrilling to do what you were doing.

522
00:41:40,880 --> 00:41:42,240
I couldn't wait to get to work.

523
00:41:42,241 --> 00:41:50,080
I can remember, oh, maybe 30 times sleeping in the back of a car because I didn't have time to go home.

524
00:41:50,600 --> 00:41:53,653
I mean, it was incredible. You just couldn't stop.

525
00:41:53,654 --> 00:41:57,560
You enjoyed doing what you were doing so much. It was thrilling.

526
00:41:58,680 --> 00:42:05,440
In March 2002, General Motors announced that the legendary Pontiac GTO will soon return,

527
00:42:05,960 --> 00:42:09,500
styled along the lines of the Australian Holden Monaro Coupe.

528
00:42:09,501 --> 00:42:16,060
Today's automakers are definitely trying to recapture the magic of the muscle car era.

529
00:42:19,900 --> 00:42:29,640
Like Model Ts and even older vehicles, muscle cars from the 1960s and 70s make us recall an era when there were no computer chips under the hood,

530
00:42:30,120 --> 00:42:35,980
no LCD readouts on speedometers, and certainly no cell phones to distract us on the road.

531
00:42:35,981 --> 00:42:40,980
All that mattered was speed and performance.

532
00:42:40,981 --> 00:42:42,980
Just plain muscle.

